{
  "result": "The library after closing is not silent.\n\nEveryone thinks it goes to sleep when the last librarian turns the key, but it doesn't. It exhales. The air conditioning clicks off and the building settles, and you can hear the faint ticking of a hundred different clocks that don't exist during the day.\n\nI used to work the night shift reshelving, and that was my favorite part. During the day, books are under pressure to perform. They have to be relevant, helpful, alphabetical. At night, they relax. A romance novel leans into a book on astrophysics. A children's picture book about dinosaurs slides slightly out, as if trying to see the exit.\n\nPaper has a smell, and at night it gets louder. Old newspapers smell like basements and iron. New paperbacks smell like clean laundry and possibility. The encyclopedias, which no one opens anymore, smell like confident dust.\n\nI never saw anything supernatural. No ghosts pulling books off shelves. But once, at 2:13 a.m., I found a copy of The Little Prince left open on a table I had definitely cleared. On the margin, in a child's handwriting, someone had written: 'Don't forget to look up.'\n\nI looked up. The ceiling had water stains that, from that angle, looked exactly like constellations.\n\nI never found out who left it.\n\nI kept the note. I taped it to my dashboard and, whenever I got stuck in traffic or bills or bad news, I did look up. It helps, strangely, more than you'd think.",
